<LessonLayout title="Catechist's Guide &mdash; Anointing of the Sick" cic="CCC 1499-1532" scripture="Jas 5:14-15; Mk 6:13; Lk 10:34">

<section class="content">

<h2>Catechist's Guide</h2>
<p class="big-question">For preparing and leading the lesson. For exclusive use of the catechist.</p>

<h3>Lesson Summary</h3>

<p><strong>Estimated duration:</strong> 50-60 minutes (with activities, 90 minutes)</p>
<p><strong>Central theme:</strong> The Anointing of the Sick is a sacrament of healing and hope, not merely a rite for the dying. Christ the physician touches the sick person, strengthens them with the Holy Spirit, unites their suffering to his redemptive Passion, and prepares them —if necessary— for the Father's house.</p>
<p><strong>Objectives:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The student understands that this sacrament is for the sick, not only for the dying (CCC 1511).</li>
<li>The student knows the biblical foundation: James 5 and the Gospels.</li>
<li>The student understands the effects of the sacrament according to the Catechism (CCC 1520-1523).</li>
<li>The student discovers the connection with Viaticum and the final sacraments.</li>
<li>The student appreciates the cultural richness: saints, art, and testimonies.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key verse:</strong> Jas 5:14-15</p>
<p><strong>Catechism references:</strong> CCC 1499-1532</p>

</section>

