<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>About St. Camillus de Lellis</h4>
<p>St. Camillus de Lellis (1550-1614) is one of the most fascinating saints in Church history. Born into a noble family in the Abruzzi region of Italy, his youth was a disaster: mercenary soldier, compulsive gambler, violent. An ulcer on his leg brought him to the Hospital of St. James in Rome, where he hit bottom —and where he found his vocation. He founded the Order of the Ministers of the Sick (the Camillians), who revolutionized hospital care with one principle: treat every sick person as if they were Christ. They invented practices now obvious but then unheard of: ventilating rooms, changing bed linens, separating contagious patients. Camillus is patron of the sick, of hospitals, and of healthcare workers. He died at 64 and his last words were: &laquo;I rejoice at having been crucified with the Lord.&raquo;</p>
<p><strong>For further study:</strong> The biography of St. Camillus by Fr. Mario Vanti offers moving details. His life is also a testimony that no one is too lost for grace.</p>

<h4>About the Anointing as a sacrament of healing</h4>
<p>It is crucial that students understand the change in perspective brought by the Second Vatican Council (Constitution <em>Sacrosanctum Concilium</em> 73). Before 1963, common practice was to call this sacrament &laquo;Extreme Unction&raquo; and administer it only in imminent danger of death —often when the sick person was already losing consciousness. The vast majority of the faithful died without having received it, or received it without knowing. The Council recovered the original name and restored its meaning: it is a sacrament for the <strong>conscious sick person</strong>, to strengthen them in their struggle, not to bid them farewell. This pastoral point is fundamental: if students leave with the idea that &laquo;the Anointing is for those who are already about to die,&raquo; they will never request it for themselves or their family members.</p>

<h4>About suffering as participation in the Cross</h4>
<p>This is perhaps the most difficult point to convey. Human suffering, in itself, is an evil. We must not glorify it or seek it. But when suffering comes —and it comes to all of us— the Christian faith offers something radically different from stoicism (&laquo;grin and bear it&raquo;) or nihilism (&laquo;it's meaningless&raquo;). It offers the possibility of <strong>uniting suffering to Christ's</strong>, transforming it into an act of redemptive love. It is not that Christ needs our pain; it is that he loves us so much that he allows us to participate in his saving work. St. Paul's letter to the Colossians (Col 1:24) is fundamental here, as is the encyclical <em>Salvifici doloris</em> of St. John Paul II (1984). Don't force this idea; propose it with tenderness.</p>

<h4>Age Adaptation</h4>
<p><strong>&#x1f331; Children (7-12):</strong> The image of &laquo;Jesus the doctor&raquo; works very well. Ask: &laquo;What does a doctor do when you're sick? He touches you, gives you medicine, tells you you'll get better. Jesus is even better: he touches you with blessed oil, gives you his strength, and never leaves you alone.&raquo; The cross activity with names of the sick is ideal for them. It can be done with crayons or markers. They can also dramatize the Good Samaritan parable.</p>
<p><strong>&#x1f33f; Youth (13-17):</strong> Connect with their experience of fragility: &laquo;Have you ever been afraid? Have you ever been alone in a hospital?&raquo; The testimony of Blessed Carlo Acutis —canonized in 2025— who offered his illness with joy, is very powerful for them. Also the story of the young Camillus, who felt like a failure before his conversion. Use the &laquo;Catequizis&raquo; video by Juan Manuel Cotelo on the Anointing (Infinito + 1).</p>
<p><strong>&#x1fab4; New adults:</strong> Skepticism about this sacrament is very common: &laquo;Isn't this only for when you're about to die?&raquo; Explain the Vatican II change clearly. The experiential argument: &laquo;When you are truly sick, what do you desire most? Someone to be with you. The Anointing is exactly that: Christ himself being with you.&raquo;</p>
<p><strong>&#x1f333; Formed adults:</strong> Deepen the theology of Christian suffering: St. Thomas's <em>desiderium naturale</em> applied to the final end, the distinction between physical healing and spiritual salvation, the concept of Viaticum as the culmination of Christian initiation. St. John Paul II and Benedict XVI (encyclical <em>Spe salvi</em>) are key references. The ecclesial dimension of suffering (CCC 1522) often sparks good discussion.</p>

<h4>About Viaticum</h4>
<p>Many Catholics have never heard this word. Explain the etymology: <em>viaticum</em> = provision for the journey. The Eucharist is food for the road, and at the final moment it is <strong>food to cross the frontier of death</strong>. It is important that students know the three final sacraments have a logical order: Penance (forgiveness), Anointing (strength), Eucharist (nourishment). It is a summary of Christian life.</p>

<h4>Supplementary Resources</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>YouCat 241-248:</strong> The Youth Catechism has a brief and clear section on the Anointing.</li>
<li><strong>Opus Dei, Theme 17:</strong> The series &laquo;Themes of the Christian Faith&raquo; from Opus Dei at opusdei.org includes the topic of the Anointing.</li>
<li><strong>Infinito + 1 (Juan Manuel Cotelo):</strong> The &laquo;Catequizis&raquo; series on YouTube. The episode on the Anointing is moving and clear.</li>
<li><strong>Ascension Presents (Fr. Mike Schmitz):</strong> Very accessible videos on this sacrament.</li>
<li><strong>Padre Luis Toro:</strong> YouTube catechesis in Spanish with direct language for youth and adults.</li>
<li><strong><em>Salvifici doloris</em> (St. John Paul II, 1984):</strong> A brief and profound apostolic letter. Recommended reading for catechists.</li>
</ul>

