<section class="content">

<h3>Possible Difficult Questions (and How to Answer Them)</h3>

<h4>&laquo;If I get anointed, does that mean I'm going to die?&raquo;</h4>
<p>No. That is precisely the idea the Church has corrected. The Anointing is not an announcement of death; it is a sacrament to ask for <strong>strength and healing</strong>. It can be received several times in one's life. Many people receive it before major surgery and then live many more years. The priest is not there to bid you farewell; he is there to bring you Christ's grace that fights alongside you.</p>

<h4>&laquo;Why doesn't God cure me? I've prayed so much and I'm still sick&raquo;</h4>
<p>This is a deeply painful question and there is no easy answer. You might say: &laquo;I don't know why God allows this particular illness to continue. But I do know that God is not indifferent to your suffering. Look at him on the cross: he didn't stay in heaven giving explanations. He came down, suffered himself, and now accompanies you. The Anointing is not a guarantee of physical cure —though that can happen— but the certainty that <strong>Christ is with you</strong> in the illness.&raquo; Do not try to resolve the mystery of suffering in three minutes. Accompany.</p>

<h4>&laquo;My relative is very ill, but I don't want to call the priest because they'll be frightened and give up&raquo;</h4>
<p>This is the most common fear. The pastoral response is clear: if the sick person is conscious, you must explain to them <strong>beforehand</strong> what the Anointing means —that it is a sacrament of life, of strength, not of death. If the sick person is unconscious, the Church permits anointing someone in danger even if they cannot express themselves. But the ideal is to prepare the sick person in time: &laquo;Dad/Mom, I'm going to call the priest to bring you the blessing of the sick. It's so God gives you strength.&raquo; The tone changes everything.</p>

<h4>&laquo;What's the difference between Anointing and just praying for the sick? Aren't they the same?&raquo;</h4>
<p>They are not the same. Private prayer is powerful and necessary. But the Anointing is a sacrament: an action of Christ himself through his Church, with an efficacy guaranteed by the Lord's promise. When I pray for a sick person, I <strong>ask</strong> God to help them. When the Church administers the Anointing, <strong>Christ himself acts</strong> through the priest. It does not depend on the faith of the minister or the sick person —though faith disposes one better. It is Christ who acts. That is why the Anointing is irreplaceable.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus, divine physician who touched lepers<br />
and gave sight to the blind,<br />
give me a heart like the Good Samaritan's:<br />
that I may know how to stop before the one who suffers,<br />
bandage their wounds with tenderness,<br />
and pour the oil of your consolation.<br />
Do not let my words drive away,<br />
but rather draw them to you.<br />
May the sick I encounter today<br />
see in me —just a little— your face.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

